## Difference from the primary proposal

I agree with the two-file repair and forced-recompilation requirement. I do not
recommend its preparatory-commit/uncommitted-implementation procedure. This
conversation consistently leaves the complete verified repair for the operator
to commit. Manuals 009 and 010 already establish the appropriate pattern: an
explicit exception to the standing committed-green acceptance rule, followed by
normal profile-1 verification. Apply that pattern to 0150 rather than prescribe
two differently scoped commits. The agent must not commit either part.

## Recurrence and prevention

This repeats 0132's inherited-defect versus slice-scope conflict and 0118's native
picker coverage issue, but neither earlier repair promised coverage of this new
adapter or future compiler warnings. Reuse and extend the earlier fixture, and
do not reopen the resolved scratch-construction diagnosis.

The warning acceptance pattern is a real process weakness. A warning-bearing
compilation followed by an up-to-date build is not a warning fix. Immediate
recovery can preserve the frozen gate contracts while requiring source repair
and forced recompilation. Longer-term prevention belongs in deterministic
project warning enforcement and retention of earlier build diagnostics for
review, rather than repeated model retries. This review does not claim ASG
mis-evaluated its configured pass condition, nor authorize a broader build-policy
or ASG change. The aggregate arc analysis should distinguish that enforcement
gap from the current product repairs.

